  "query_preview": "..\nThe upper pattern this morning is defined by a strong ridge of \nhigh pressure extending from New Mexico all the way over to \nsouthern Appalachia with two distinct high centers over central \nNew Mexico and central Tennessee with an inverted trough in \nbetween these two features over Texas. The latest GOES \nprecipitable water imagery still indicated a moist airmass \nacross Southeast Arizona with the deeper moisture residing from \nTucson west (PWATs between 1.60 and 2.00 inches) and PWAT values\n1.40 to 1.60 inches east of Tucson into SW New Mexico and NW \nChihuahua MX. This airmass is relatively moist for this time of \nyear...generally between 1.5 and 2.0 standard deviations above \nnormal. \n\nThat inverted trough over Texas will track west today shoving\nthe upper-high center over central New Mexico into central \nArizona by 10/00Z this afternoon, then over to western Arizona \nby 10/06Z this evening. This movement will result in an area of \ndecent stretching deformation/lift aloft across the SE half of \nthe CWA today. Although the upper pattern will still be a \nfavored rim-shot pattern to move storms from the Mogollon Rim \ntoward our neck of the woods, the elongated nature of the upper \nhigh will result in a more NNE-to-SSW than NE-to-SW trajectory \nof storms essentially shunting the best area of lift to the east\nand south of Tucson today. Models have slightly backed off on \nthe 0-6km AGL Bulk Shear (20 kts) today...but this should still \nbe enough to help organize and cluster some of the generally \npulse-type thunderstorms today as they track SSW. The anomalously\nhigh moisture available across the area will provide the potential\nfor heavy rainfall/isolated flash flooding today. This is\nsupported by WPC's area of slight risk for exceeding flash \nflood guidance on their Day 1 Excessive rainfall Outlook (ERO) \nextending across the eastern 2/3 of Southeast Arizona today. \nSo, the primary hazard for storms today will be the potential \nfor heavy rain/isolated flash flooding (especially to the \nsoutheast and south of Tucson) with a secondary threat for \nstrong thunderstorm outflow winds/isolated severe outflow winds \n(east of Tucson). The Tucson Metro Area will be on the periphery\nof both of these hazard areas today due to the SSW trajectory \nof the flow aloft.\n\nOtherwise, we will maintain enough moisture across the region \nfor mainly scattered afternoon and early evening thunderstorms \nthis weekend into early next week. The Monsoon season rolls-on.",
